{
  "dom_id": "step-q50910",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n!}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the Taylor series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$ \n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(-1)^{n-1}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)x\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{|x|}{n+1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$  Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that \n$R=\\infty .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
